Make the interpreter repo relocatable

python_repository writes a convenience symlink "python" that points at the
interpreter.  It creates the link with rctx.symlink(), which turns the relative
target "bin/python3" into an absolute path.  At fetch time the repo still lives
in the output base, so the link becomes:

    <output_base>/external/rules_python++python+python_3_14_...-gnu/bin/python3

With --repo_contents_cache Bazel then moves the repo directory into the shared
cache (RepoContentsCache.moveToCache) and leaves a symlink behind at the old
path.  It does not rewrite or validate symlinks inside the repo, so the cached
copy keeps a back-reference into the output base that fetched it.  Every other
workspace that reuses the entry depends on that output base.  If a user deletes
that workspace, the link dangles and all other workspaces fail with:

    Error: .../_bazel_<user>/<hash> (No such file or directory)

Bazel cannot recover from this, because the recorded inputs of the entry hold
only environment variables and a repo mapping.  Nothing records the symlink
target, so no refetch is triggered.

Write a relative link instead.  It resolves from any output base, which is what
a shared cache entry requires.  ln stores the target verbatim and resolves it
against the directory of the link, so the absolute link path is safe here.

Windows keeps rctx.symlink(), because ln is not available there.

diff --git a/python/private/python_repository.bzl b/python/private/python_repository.bzl
--- a/python/private/python_repository.bzl
+++ b/python/private/python_repository.bzl
@@ -292,7 +292,17 @@
         coverage_tool = render.str(coverage_tool),
     )
     rctx.delete("python")
-    rctx.symlink(python_bin, "python")
+    if repo_utils.get_platforms_os_name(rctx) == "windows":
+        rctx.symlink(python_bin, "python")
+    else:
+        # rctx.symlink() absolutizes the target, which pins the repo to the
+        # output base that fetched it once the repo contents cache shares it.
+        repo_utils.execute_checked(
+            rctx,
+            op = "SymlinkPythonRelative",
+            arguments = [repo_utils.which_checked(rctx, "ln"), "-s", python_bin, str(rctx.path("python"))],
+            logger = logger,
+        )
     rctx.file(STANDALONE_INTERPRETER_FILENAME, "# File intentionally left blank. Indicates that this is an interpreter repo created by rules_python.")
     rctx.file("BUILD.bazel", build_content)
 
